        "I am therefore fully aware of the resistances I am bound to encounter and the criticisms to which I lay myself open. I must say, however, that the terminology in question seems to me the only one adequate to the body of insights I propose to sketch, and whose importance is, in my eyes, central. If there are any readers of my Metaphysical Journal here, they will have no difficulty in observing that the fundamental theses I am going to put forward are the culmination of the entire philosophical and spiritual evolution pursued through the Journal. Rather than setting out immediately from abstract definitions and dialectical arguments calculated to discourage my listeners at the very start, I should like to present at the outset a sort of global and intuitive characterization of what a man is who lacks the ontological sense, the sense of being, or more precisely who has lost consciousness of possessing it. In a general way modern man is like this, and if the ontological exigence still works within him, it is dully, like a blind thrust. I wonder whether a psychoanalysis more delicate and profound than any developed up to now might not succeed in detecting the morbid effects engendered by the repression of this sense, of this unrecognized demand.",
        
