        "It often happens that I ask myself with a sort of anxiety what can be the life or the inner reality of, for instance, a subway worker—the man who opens the doors, or the man who punches the tickets. It must be admitted that both within him and outside him everything conspires to bring about the identification of this man with his functions—not only his function as employee, or trade unionist, or voter, but also his vital functions. The fundamentally dreadful expression 'timetable' (emploi du temps) finds its full application here. So many hours are allotted to such and such functions. Sleep, too, is a function that must be discharged so that other functions may be discharged in turn. And it is the same with recreation, with relaxation. We can easily imagine a hygienist coming along to state that a man needs so many hours of entertainment per week. This is an organico-psychic function that can no more be neglected, I suppose, than sexual functions, for instance. It is useless to labor the point; this sketch is sufficient. We see taking shape here the idea of a sort of biological schedule (barème vital) whose details naturally vary according to countries, climates, occupations, etc. But what matters is that there is a schedule.",
        
